= Reading The City

Walking makes the city legible through encounters that no map can fully
anticipate.#deixis-endnote[
  Michel de Certeau, _The Practice of Everyday Life_, trans. Steven Rendall
  (Berkeley: University of California Press, 1984), 91–93.
] The street is also a social institution: its apparent disorder can sustain a
surprisingly intricate public life.#deixis-endnote[
  Jane Jacobs, _The Death and Life of Great American Cities_
  (New York: Random House, 1961), 50–54.
]

Such accounts invite us to read ordinary places as historical evidence.
A building's meaning emerges from its uses as well as from its planned
form.#deixis-endnote[
  Dolores Hayden, _The Power of Place: Urban Landscapes as Public History_
  (Cambridge, MA: MIT Press, 1995), 18–20.
] Returning to the street, we can understand walking as a practice that both
uses and revises the city's inherited order.#deixis-endnote[
  De Certeau, _Practice of Everyday Life_, 97–99.
]

